 function on different inputs and count as separate logical nodes. If I intervene only on my own input under Equation (4), the other copy's action stays fixed, which would make defecting look dominant, unless the graph links the two inputs.\n\nI'm checking whether the two copies could count as one node if their inputs are relevantly identical, but I'm treating them as different inputs under the strict reading. With off-input priors held at symmetry, defect comes out better in the fixed-point check (about 1 to 2 years less), so the strict reading lands on mutual defection. The essay's counterpossible covariance point is the main argument against that, and I'm weighing it next.\n\nI'm still weighing the counterpossible covariance point: if the other copy's output tracks mine across variations, the two could count as one node. But the essay holds the off-input output at its prior and evaluates only my actual input, so I'm leaning toward defect, since the fixed point is unique and defect dominates regardless of the prior.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through a twin prisoner's dilemma where the two copies receive different observations, so under A-FDT they're running the same function on different inputs and count as separate logical nodes. If I intervene only on my own input under Equation (4), the other copy's action stays fixed, which would make defecting look dominant, unless the graph links the two inputs.\n\nI'm checking whether the two copies could count as one node if their inputs are relevantly identical, but I'm treating them as different inputs under the strict reading. With off-input priors held at symmetry, defect comes out better in the fixed-point check (about 1 to 2 years less), so the strict reading lands on mutual defection. The essay's counterpossible covariance point is the main argument against that, and I'm weighing it next.\n\nI'm still weighing the counterpossible covariance point: if the other copy's output tracks mine across variations, the two could count as one node. But the essay holds the off-input output at its prior and evaluates only my actual input, so I'm leaning toward defect, since the fixed point is unique and defect dominates regardless of the prior.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10057,"completion_tokens":1312,"total_tokens":11369,"cost":0.066468,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.066468,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.040228,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02624},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":734,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}